= i
{parent=4}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=i}

Fix $z\in K$. If $z\in\partial K$, the conclusion is immediate. Otherwise $z$ is in the <interior> of $K$. If $f(z)=0$, nonnegativity of the <boundary> <supremum> suffices. For $f(z)\ne0$, the complex <Hahn-Banach theorem> gives a <bounded linear functional> $\ell:A\to\mathbb C$ with $\|\ell\|=1$ and $\ell(f(z))=\|f(z)\|$. The scalar function $g=\ell\circ f$ is <holomorphic>.

Let $V$ be the <connected component> of the <interior> of $K$ containing $z$. It is bounded, its closure is contained in $K$, and $\partial V\subseteq\partial K$. To see the last inclusion, a <boundary> point of $V$ lying in the <interior> of $K$ would have a small <connected> open ball in that <interior> meeting $V$; the ball would belong to the same component, contradicting that it is a <boundary> point. The scalar <maximum modulus principle on a bounded domain> therefore gives
$$
\|f(z)\|=|g(z)|\le\sup_{w\in\partial V}|g(w)|\le\sup_{w\in\partial K}\|f(w)\|.
$$
For <completeness>, this <boundary> form of the <maximum modulus principle> follows by taking the maximum on the <compact> closure of $V$. An <interior> maximum makes $g$ constant on $V$, in which case <continuity> gives the same value on its nonempty <boundary>. Thus the <norm maximum principle for a Banach-space-valued holomorphic function> applies to arbitrary <compact> $K$, even when it is disconnected or has nonsmooth <boundary>:
$$
\boxed{\|f(z)\|\le\sup_{w\in\partial K}\|f(w)\|.}
$$

= ii
{parent=4}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=ii}

For $n\ge0$, put
$$
p_n(w)=\|f(w)^{2^n}\|^{1/2^n},\qquad M=\sup_{w\in\partial K}r(f(w)).
$$
The bound $r(u)\le\|u\|$ makes $M$ finite. Each $p_n$ is <continuous>; <submultiplicativity> gives $p_{n+1}\le p_n$, and the <spectral radius formula> gives $p_n(w)\to r(f(w))$. <Spectral radius> need not be <continuous>, so applying the <Dini theorem> directly to $p_n$ would not be justified. Instead define, on the <compact> <boundary>,
$$
q_n(w)=\max\{p_n(w),M\}.
$$
These are <continuous>, decrease pointwise to the <continuous> constant $M$, and therefore converge uniformly by the <Dini theorem>. In particular,
$$
\limsup_{n\to\infty}\sup_{w\in\partial K}p_n(w)\le M.
$$
Multiplication is a <continuous> bilinear operation in a <Banach algebra>, so the <product rule> shows that $w\mapsto f(w)^{2^n}$ is a <Banach-space-valued holomorphic function>. Part (i), applied to this power, gives
$$
\|f(z)^{2^n}\|^{1/2^n}\le\sup_{w\in\partial K}\|f(w)^{2^n}\|^{1/2^n}.
$$
Taking the limit on the left and the limit superior on the right proves the <spectral-radius maximum principle>:
$$
\boxed{r(f(z))\le\sup_{w\in\partial K}r(f(w)).}
$$

= Solution
{parent=4}

Apply the <spectral-radius maximum principle> to the entire <Banach-space-valued holomorphic function> $f(z)=a+zb$ on the <closed> disk $|z|\le\varepsilon$. On its <boundary> the assumed estimate gives $r(f(z))\le C\varepsilon$. Therefore
$$
0\le r(a)=r(f(0))\le C\varepsilon
$$
for every $\varepsilon>0$. Letting $\varepsilon\downarrow0$ proves
$$
\boxed{r(a)=0.}
$$
The puncture at $z=0$ in the hypothesis causes no difficulty: the bound is used only on the nonzero <boundary> circle.

= 5
{parent=Paper 7}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=5}

An <algebra involution> is a map $x\mapsto x^*$ satisfying
$$
(\alpha x+\beta y)^*=\overline\alpha x^*+\overline\beta y^*,\qquad
(xy)^*=y^*x^*,\qquad (x^*)^*=x.
$$
Thus it is a <conjugate-linear map>, reverses multiplication, and has square equal to the identity map. These are algebraic axioms; <norm> <continuity>, when included in a convention for a Banach star algebra, is an additional requirement and is not used here. A <Hermitian element of a star algebra> satisfies $h^*=h$.

Conjugate <linearity> gives $0^*=0$. The map $*$ is surjective, so every $u$ is $v^*$ for some $v$. The identities $(v1)^*=1^*v^*=v^*$ and $(1v)^*=v^*1^*=v^*$ show that $1^*$ is a two-sided identity; uniqueness of the identity gives $1^*=1$. Therefore **both zero and the identity are <Hermitian algebra elements>**.

For the final unheaded request, first prove equality of the two spectra for a <Hermitian element of a star algebra> $h\in B$. Write $D=\mathbb C\setminus\sigma_A(h)$. The spectrum is a <compact> subset of the real axis by the assumption that $A$ is a <Hermitian Banach algebra>, so $D$ is <connected>: points above and below the real axis can be joined by paths passing beyond the endpoints of an interval containing the spectrum; points of the real axis outside the spectrum can first move a short distance vertically. Define
$$
E=\{\lambda\in D:(\lambda1-h)^{-1}\in B\}.
$$
For large $|\lambda|$, the <Neumann series> $\lambda^{-1}\sum_{n\ge0}(h/\lambda)^n$ converges in the <closed> unital <star subalgebra> $B$, so $E$ is nonempty. It is relatively open in $D$ by the same series applied to a perturbation of an already invertible element of $B$. It is relatively <closed> in $D$, since the <resolvent of an element> is <continuous> in $A$ and $B$ is <closed>. Connectedness gives $E=D$. Conversely an inverse in $B$ is an inverse in $A$, so
$$
\sigma_B(h)=\sigma_A(h).
$$
This proves the needed <spectrum in a closed unital subalgebra> equality for <Hermitian algebra elements> without presupposing that their spectra in $B$ are real.

Now suppose $b\in B$ is invertible in $A$. Both $bb^*$ and $b^*b$ are <Hermitian algebra elements>, since reversing products and applying $*$ twice fixes them. They are invertible in $A$, by part (ii), so the preceding equality of spectra of <Hermitian algebra elements> makes them invertible in $B$. Part (ii), applied within $B$, then makes $b$ invertible in $B$. Applying this to $b-\lambda1$ for every scalar $\lambda$, and using the automatic converse for an inverse already in $B$, proves <spectral permanence for Hermitian Banach algebras>:
$$
\boxed{\sigma_B(b)=\sigma_A(b)\quad(b\in B).}
$$
Only closedness, a common identity, closure under the <algebra involution>, and real ambient spectra of <Hermitian algebra elements> were used; the <C-star identity> was not assumed.

= i
{parent=5}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=i}

If $u$ is invertible, applying the <algebra involution> to $u^{-1}u=uu^{-1}=1$ gives
$$
u^*(u^{-1})^*=(u^{-1})^*u^*=1,
$$
so $u^*$ is invertible and $(u^*)^{-1}=(u^{-1})^*$. The converse follows by applying $*$ again. Since
$$
(x-\lambda1)^*=x^*-\overline\lambda1,
$$
we have $\lambda\notin\sigma_A(x)$ if and only if $\overline\lambda\notin\sigma_A(x^*)$. Taking complements and using that <complex conjugation> is a bijection gives
$$
\boxed{\sigma_A(x^*)=\{\overline\lambda:\lambda\in\sigma_A(x)\}.}
$$
The conjugation bar in the printed PDF is essential; this is not an assertion that the two spectra always coincide.

= ii
{parent=5}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=ii}

If $x$ is invertible, part (i)'s inverse calculation gives an inverse for $x^*$, and therefore both $xx^*$ and $x^*x$ are invertible. Conversely, if both products are invertible, set
$$
r=x^*(xx^*)^{-1},\qquad \ell=(x^*x)^{-1}x^*.
$$
Then $xr=1$ and $\ell x=1$: $r$ is a <right inverse of an algebra element> $x$ and $\ell$ is a <left inverse of an algebra element> $x$. They coincide because $\ell=\ell(xr)=(\ell x)r=r$, providing a two-sided inverse. Thus
$$
\boxed{x\text{ invertible}\iff xx^*\text{ and }x^*x\text{ both invertible},\qquad x^{-1}=x^*(xx^*)^{-1}=(x^*x)^{-1}x^*.}
$$
Both products are required in a general <Banach algebra>; a single one-sided inverse need not suffice.
